---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import DisplayTitle from '../components/DisplayTitle.astro';

const principles = [
  {
    n: 1,
    title: 'National Sovereignty Is the Foundation, Not the Obstacle',
    short: 'I propose shared tools, not a shared government. Each member state retains the right to decide what matters to it.',
  },
  {
    n: 2,
    title: 'Shared Interest, Not Shared Burden',
    short: 'We do together what every country gains from individually — and where economies of scale make the result dramatically better. We do not pool what is a cost for some and a benefit for others.',
  },
  {
    n: 3,
    title: 'A Direction, Not a Specification',
    short: 'I propose three pillars — language, digitisation, robotisation. The exact shape of each system is for those who will implement it.',
  },
  {
    n: 4,
    title: 'Synthesis Builds Culture; Force Cannot',
    short: 'A common European culture is valuable, but it can only emerge from cooperation. Every attempt to legislate it has produced performance, not culture.',
  },
  {
    n: 5,
    title: 'Additive Reform — New Programmes, Old Institutions Stay',
    short: 'Nothing I propose requires dismantling existing EU or national institutions. Every reform is a new programme that countries can join when ready, alongside what already works.',
  },
  {
    n: 6,
    title: 'Positivism as Method — Build, Don\'t Administer',
    short: 'In the AI era, the work that remains for humans is creation, not administration. Build new infrastructure rather than redistribute resources within old frameworks.',
  },
];
---

<Layout title="Principles">
  <main class="container mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4 py-8">
    <DisplayTitle title="Principles" />

    <div class="mb-10 text-xl" style="color: var(--color-text-secondary);">
      Six principles that define what kind of European reform I propose — and what I do not.
      They are deliberately few, deliberately specific, and they stand apart from both Brussels federalism
      and Eurosceptic rejection. Read these before you read anything else on this site.
    </div>

    <!-- Six principle cards: 2 per row, 3 rows -->
    <div class="grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 mb-16">
      {principles.map(p => (
        <div class="p-6 rounded-lg shadow-md transition-colors duration-300"
             style="background-color: var(--color-bg-secondary); border: 1px solid var(--color-border);">
          <div class="flex items-center mb-4">
            <div class="flex-shrink-0 rounded-full w-10 h-10 flex items-center justify-center mr-3"
                 style="background: linear-gradient(to bottom right, var(--color-accent-primary), var(--color-accent-secondary));">
              <span class="text-xl font-bold text-white">{p.n}</span>
            </div>
            <h3 class="font-bold text-base leading-tight" style="color: var(--color-text-primary);">{p.title}</h3>
          </div>
          <p class="text-sm" style="color: var(--color-text-secondary);">{p.short}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <!-- Deep dive sections -->

    <section class="mb-16">
      <DisplayTitle level={2} title="1. National sovereignty is the foundation" class="mb-6" />
      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none" style="color: var(--color-text-secondary);">
        <p>
          The reform I propose is not a step toward a federal European super-state. It is a proposal for a small number of <strong>shared tools</strong> — language, digital infrastructure, robotic capacity — that make cooperation between sovereign nations easier and cheaper.
        </p>
        <p>
          Member states keep the right to decide what they care about. Education, taxation, social policy, cultural life — these belong to nations. The reform makes it easier for nations to cooperate where they want to, not easier for Brussels to impose what nations do not want.
        </p>
        <p style="color: var(--color-text-primary);">
          <strong>Why this matters:</strong> any reform that ignores sovereignty fails politically, and rightly so. I would rather propose less and have it accepted than propose everything and have nothing happen.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-16">
      <DisplayTitle level={2} title="2. Shared interest, not shared burden" class="mb-6" />
      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none" style="color: var(--color-text-secondary);">
        <p>
          This is the line that separates what we should do together from what we should not.
        </p>
        <p style="color: var(--color-text-primary);"><strong>Do together:</strong> things every country gains from individually, especially where economies of scale make the result dramatically better.</p>
        <p>
          The European Union's greatest successes — the single market, free movement, common research programmes — all relied on the same insight: certain things become dramatically cheaper, better, or possible at all when done at scale. The next phase of integration applies the same logic to the digital economy.
        </p>
        <p>
          The clearest example is <a href="/vision/digital-integration" style="color: var(--color-accent-primary);">predictive healthcare powered by shared anonymised medical data</a>. Every aging society in Europe needs better early diagnosis. An AI model trained on data from 450 million Europeans is dramatically better than one trained on 5, 10 or 30 million. The model is shared. Each national health system uses it locally. No country pays for another's care — every country saves money and saves lives.
        </p>
        <p>
          The pandemic taught the same lesson the hard way. Twenty-seven EU countries each built their own e-learning system from scratch. The modules — gradebook, video classroom, chat, attendance, parent portal — were near-identical. The waste was in the hundreds of millions of euros, possibly billions. One shared open-source backbone with national customisation on top would have cost a small fraction and produced better software for everyone.
        </p>
        <p>
          Other examples in this category: a unified payment zone, common defence R&D, robotic logistics standards, shared border-control technology, a European cloud for public-sector data, common digital identity layer.
        </p>
        <p style="color: var(--color-text-primary);"><strong>Do not pool:</strong> things that are a cost for some and a benefit for others.</p>
        <p>
          A common European pension system would be such a thing. Some countries have saved (Scandinavia, Germany), some have not. A pooled scheme would mean transferring savings — and the savers will refuse, correctly. The same applies to debt mutualisation, harmonised taxation, or any other "let us put money together" proposal. They are politically poisonous and they should be — they ask one country to subsidise another's choices.
        </p>
        <p>
          Note the line: we can share the <em>technology</em> of pension administration (predictive solvency models, demographic forecasting tools, automated benefit calculation) without sharing the <em>fund</em>. The technology is shared interest. The fund is shared burden. Same domain, opposite verdicts.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-16">
      <DisplayTitle level={2} title="3. A direction, not a specification" class="mb-6" />
      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none" style="color: var(--color-text-secondary);">
        <p>
          I am not writing a policy paper with paragraph numbers. I am proposing a direction:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc ml-6 my-4">
          <li>A common European working language (English as the second official language of the EU)</li>
          <li>A common digital backbone for public administration</li>
          <li>Common robotic and automation capacity for public services and defence</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The exact shape of each system — which protocols, which suppliers, which timelines — must be worked out by the people who will implement it. I am an engineer; I know that the difference between a good system and a bad one is in details that no manifesto can decide in advance.
        </p>
        <p style="color: var(--color-text-primary);">
          <strong>What this means in practice:</strong> if you find a paragraph on this site that proposes a specific implementation detail and you disagree with it, fine. The detail is not the point. The direction is.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-16">
      <DisplayTitle level={2} title="4. Synthesis builds culture; force cannot" class="mb-6" />
      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none" style="color: var(--color-text-secondary);">
        <p>
          A common European culture — a shared way of debating, working, and building things — is something I consider valuable. It would be a real achievement and a long-term goal worth pursuing. I do not want this misread as an Eurosceptic dismissal of common culture. It is the opposite.
        </p>
        <p>
          But common culture cannot be created by decree, harmonisation directive, or any other form of imposition from above. Every time European institutions have tried this — through cultural campaigns, mandated curricula, or symbolic legislation — the result has been not common culture but a hollow performance of common culture. That performance feeds Eurosceptic backlash and makes real cooperation harder. Force does not produce culture; it produces pose.
        </p>
        <p>
          Real culture only ever emerges from <strong>synthesis</strong>. People working together produce shared vocabulary. People trading together produce shared norms. People building things together produce shared standards of what counts as good work. This is how every cultural community in history has actually formed — by participation, never by fiat.
        </p>
        <p style="color: var(--color-text-primary);"><strong>What this means in practice:</strong></p>
        <ul class="list-disc ml-6 my-4">
          <li>A common working language (English) creates the conditions for shared debate. It does not produce the debate; the debate emerges when there is something to debate about.</li>
          <li>A common digital administration creates the conditions for shared business culture. It does not impose how business is done; that emerges from millions of cross-border transactions running on the same infrastructure.</li>
          <li>Fast continental logistics creates the conditions for a shared trading culture. A package from Warsaw to Lisbon arriving in two days does more for European identity than any cultural directive ever has.</li>
          <li>Joint European projects — predictive healthcare, defence R&D, robotic logistics — create the conditions for a shared engineering culture, built by Europeans solving real problems together.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Whether these conditions ultimately produce a recognisable "European culture" in some thicker sense — common heroes, shared narratives, mutual literary references — is for future generations to discover. Our job is to build the conditions, not to dictate the result. Culture identifies itself, eventually. We do not get to specify it in advance.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is, in part, the Polish contribution to the debate: do not give up on common culture, but refuse to fake it. Build the conditions, respect the diversity, let synthesis do its work across generations.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-16">
      <DisplayTitle level={2} title="5. Additive reform — new programmes, old institutions stay" class="mb-6" />
      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none" style="color: var(--color-text-secondary);">
        <p>
          The biggest fear any proposal for European reform triggers, especially in countries with strong national identities, is the fear of being dismantled. "If we agree to this, what we have will be taken away from us."
        </p>
        <p>
          I want to be unambiguous about this: <strong>nothing I propose requires dismantling existing institutions.</strong> The Commission, Council, and Parliament keep their current functions. National courts, ministries, and social systems keep their current sovereignty. Every reform on this site is additive.
        </p>
        <p style="color: var(--color-text-primary);"><strong>What additive means in practice:</strong></p>
        <ul class="list-disc ml-6 my-4">
          <li><strong>A common working language</strong> sits alongside national languages. No one is asked to give up Polish, French, or German. English becomes the second official language for cross-border work, not the only one.</li>
          <li><strong>Aurora</strong> (a basket-currency layer for cross-border settlements) sits above existing national currencies and the Euro. No one is forced to give up the złoty, the krone, or the Euro. Member States that choose to use Aurora for European-scale transactions can do so without ever joining the Euro.</li>
          <li><strong>A European Health AI Initiative</strong> is a new programme. National health ministries keep their authority over national systems. The shared AI model is infrastructure that any country can plug into, on its own terms.</li>
          <li><strong>A common digital identity layer</strong> sits alongside national IDs. Estonian e-Residency does not replace a passport; the European layer would not either.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This is evolution, not revolution. Lower political cost (countries can join when ready). Lower implementation risk (you can start small). Reversible (you can stop using a programme without dismantling anything).
        </p>
        <p>
          A second benefit: this addresses the legitimate Eurosceptic argument that integration is always one-way and irreversible. With additive reform, joining a programme is a positive choice and not joining is also a positive choice. The European institutional landscape becomes a menu of options rather than a single package to swallow whole.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-16">
      <DisplayTitle level={2} title="6. Positivism as method — build, don't administer" class="mb-6" />
      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none" style="color: var(--color-text-secondary);">
        <p>
          The philosophical source of this method is Polish positivism. After the failed January Uprising of 1863, Polish intellectuals — denied a state of their own under three foreign occupations — made a deliberate choice. They stopped fighting for what did not yet exist and started building what they could build now. They founded scientific funds, vocational schools, rural libraries, journals, factories. Not declarations. Institutions. They called it <em>praca organiczna</em> — organic work — and they meant it literally: a society as a body whose every functional part has to actually function. By 1918, when Poland regained independence, the country existed as an organism even though it had not existed as a state for 123 years. That is what builders can do that complainers cannot.
        </p>
        <p>
          The method translates directly to European reform in 2026, with a sharper edge that fits our century:
        </p>
        <p style="color: var(--color-text-primary);">
          <strong>1. Build, do not administer.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          The work of administration — moving money from one budget line to another, redistributing resources within an existing framework, managing what already exists — is the work that artificial intelligence and automation will absorb first. It is exactly the kind of pattern-following, rule-applying labour that algorithms do well. The work that remains for humans is creation: new systems, new institutions, new tools that did not exist before.
        </p>
        <p>
          A reformer in 2026 who proposes redistribution is proposing a job that algorithms will do better in five years. A reformer who proposes building something new is proposing the only kind of work that will still be human. This is not a political argument about left and right; it is a structural argument about what European labour will look like.
        </p>
        <p style="color: var(--color-text-primary);">
          <strong>2. Create efficient solutions; then invest in them.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Pouring money into solving problems with outdated methods is a category error. The same euro spent on automating an inefficient process produces a result a thousand times better than the same euro spent on running that process by hand for another year. The public debate is too often framed as "more spending or less spending"; the real choice is "build or maintain."
        </p>
        <p>
          Whether redistribution stays in European policy in some form is, at this level, a secondary question. The primary question is whether we are creating better solutions for the problems that the redistribution is supposed to address. Redistribution without creation is bailing water out of a leaking boat. Creation makes the boat watertight.
        </p>
        <p style="color: var(--color-text-primary);">
          <strong>3. Identify the lowest-hanging fruit.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Things that are obviously broken, obviously fixable, that almost everyone could agree on if proposed clearly. A package from China to Poland arrives faster than a letter from Poland to Portugal. Twenty-seven national e-learning platforms were built from scratch during the pandemic. A Polish engineer working in Germany has to fight German bureaucracy in German. These problems do not need a constitutional convention. They need someone to point at them and say: <em>let us fix this one.</em>
        </p>
        <p style="color: var(--color-text-primary);">
          <strong>4. Show the working prototype before asking for the policy.</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          The most credible advocate of any reform is someone who has shown that it works. I would rather see one functioning European programme that nobody can argue with than ten elegant proposals that never ship. Ship, measure, iterate, scale. The same logic that made open-source software win the internet can make European public infrastructure work.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is what Polish positivism contributes to the European debate, and what Poland — for too long cast as the blocker and the complainer — can credibly offer that other countries cannot. A nation built scientific institutions while it had no state. We can build European infrastructure while waiting for Brussels to agree.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Closing synthesis -->
    <section class="mb-16 p-8 rounded-xl"
             style="background-color: var(--color-bg-secondary); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent-primary);">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed" style="color: var(--color-text-primary);">
        Read together, these six principles define a particular kind of European reform: <strong>ambitious in direction, modest in mechanism, respectful of sovereignty, additive rather than substitutive, positive rather than reactive.</strong> They are the conditions under which I think the European Union can survive the next twenty years. They are also the conditions under which I, as a Pole and an engineer, am willing to advocate for it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Call to Action -->
    <div class="mt-12 text-center">
      <a href="/vision" class="inline-block px-8 py-4 rounded-lg font-medium transition-colors duration-300"
         style="background: linear-gradient(to right, var(--color-accent-primary), var(--color-accent-secondary)); color: white;">
        See the Full Vision
      </a>
    </div>
  </main>
</Layout>
